{"findings": [{"file": "src/completion_queue.zig", "line": 383, "severity": "major", "category": "docs", "title": "isClosed() cannot tell a real close from a spurious win; the documented recipe stops draining while operations are still in flight", "body": "The new doc comment tells a select driver to use `isClosed()` to tell "a real close (stop draining)" from a spurious win. Only one direction of that test is sound. `closed` is monotonic, so `isClosed() == false` does imply the spurious case, as the second sentence says. But `isClosed() == true` does NOT imply the queue is drained.

`close()` is thread-safe and explicitly leaves in-flight operations running: see the contract at line 146 and the test "close over an in-flight operation does not wake a parked select" (line 1240). `wait`/`timedWait` return `error.Closed` only when `closed and pending.isEmpty() and completed.pop() == null` (lines 185, 218). `getResult` (line 302) applies no such test: it returns `error.Closed` whenever `completed` is momentarily empty. So the reachable state is: another task called `close()`, one operation is still in flight, a stale wake wins the queue arm, `getResult` returns `error.Closed`, and `isClosed()` returns true. A driver that follows the parenthetical stops draining there. The module doc (lines 12-20) says the driver owns the memory of operations that other tasks submitted and frees each one as `next` hands it out, so an early teardown loses those results and leaks the heap-backed completions.

The predicate the driver actually needs is the drained state, which the queue already computes internally in `isReady` (line 325) but does not expose. A caller cannot rebuild it from the public API in one atomic step: `isClosed()`, `hasPending()` and `hasCompleted()` each take and drop the mutex separately. Either expose the drained predicate, or restate the comment so it claims only the direction that holds: `isClosed() == false` means spurious, `isClosed() == true` means nothing on its own."}, {"file": "src/completion_queue.zig", "line": 386, "severity": "minor", "category": "tests", "title": "New public isClosed() has no test and no caller in the tree", "body": "`isClosed` is a new public method on `CompletionQueue`. Nothing in the tree calls it: the only other `isClosed` is the private one in `src/group.zig:113`. No test covers it, so none of the properties its own doc comment rests on is pinned: that it reads true after `close()`, that it stays false while the queue is open (which is the whole point of the spurious-win test), and that it stays true across the idempotent second `close()`. The existing test "wait on a closed empty queue returns error.Closed" (line 575) already asserts `isEmpty`, `hasPending` and `hasCompleted` around a close and is the natural place to add the assertions. Without one, a later change to when `closed` is set would break the documented select recipe with a green suite."}]}
